course: dictionary
lesson: 7491
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Many words have more than one meaning, and post is a good example. The words around it tell you which meaning the writer means. This is the context, and it leaves clues.
explain: «A theme park posts its wait time, 45 minutes, so you can choose another ride.» Here posts means puts up information where people can read it. «Noah forgot to log out and his account posted «I LOVE BROCCOLI» in a chat» means sent a message online. Both use posted or posts as verbs, but the context shows the action.
explain: Post can also be a noun: a strong pole, letters and parcels, or a job. Try each meaning in the sentence. The one that still makes sense is the right one.
fact: Post has several meanings, so the context tells you which one is right.
fact: A post is a strong pole stuck in the ground, such as one that holds up a fence.
fact: In the UK and Australia, the post is the mail: letters and parcels sent and delivered.
fact: To post something online means to put it on a website.
fact: On a straight fence, there is always one more post than there are gaps between posts.
exercise: «The post arrives at nine o'clock with the letters and parcels.» What does post mean here? | A strong pole stuck in the ground | *The mail: letters and parcels | To put a message on a website | A place where someone stands on duty
exercise: «Headlines are written to grab you, so read the story underneath; a shocking post that is on no trusted news site is probably fake.» What does post mean here? | A strong pole in the ground | *An item or message put on a website | A job standing on duty | Letters and parcels delivered to your door
exercise: A straight fence has 5 gaps between equally spaced posts. How many posts are there? | 7 | 5 | *6 | 4
test: «A theme park posts its wait time, 45 minutes, so you can choose another ride.» What does posts mean here? | Hammers a pole into the ground | *Puts the information up where people can see it | Stands on guard at the gate | Delivers letters to people's homes
test: Your friend says, «Keep me posted.» What does she want? | *Please tell me the news | Please send me a parcel | Please put a pole in the ground | Please stand guard at the gate
exam: A guard stands at the gate because it is his post. What does post mean here? | To put a message online | *A job or place where someone stands on duty | A strong pole in the ground | A letter sent by mail
